export const body = "<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n\n<ul><li>Uncharted territory</li><li>Experience &amp; the lack of it</li><li>Pressure makes diamonds</li><li>Limitations breed creativity</li><li>A joint effort</li><li>Inside the studio</li><li>Quality first</li><li>Closing</li></ul>\n\n<p>The book of solution was only one piece of the puzzle. The continued success of the project stems from mutual support and a strong commitment to quality.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/aab0e5546b6e81436086.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/aab0e5546b6e81436086.webp\" width=\"1117\" height=\"541\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Uncharted territory</h3>\n\n<p>Since the project is a long-term project with multiple studios, communication was a headache. The channels were limited; Art Producers were not full-time; our vendors only contacted via a few emails in the first 6 – 7 months.</p>\n\n<p>Besides, the project started without a defined leadership. Although the client suggested the studio be at the helm, our group had no pre-existing framework for overseeing multiple studios at once.</p>\n\n<h3>Experience &amp; the lack of it</h3>\n\n<p>“We claimed we have VR experience, but we don’t. We only have VR assets experience, not VR level.”</p>\n\n<p><strong>THE FIRST OBSTACLE:</strong></p>\n\n<p>In VR, players can view assets in extreme closeness from all angles, which makes it harder to cut corners like we usually do in 3D games. Therefore, the pixel density per asset had to be increased, with nothing occluded.</p>\n\n<p><strong>→</strong> Just like <strong>TV screen vs. Theater</strong> <strong>screen</strong>: low resolution will be less jarring on a TV than on a theater screen.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/736b6fa705c676eb3975.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/736b6fa705c676eb3975.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"344\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>THE SECOND OBSTACLE:</strong></p>\n\n<p>Another obstacle was the lack of art direction from the client's side. They only assigned one Creative Director and one Brief Specialist to provide us with historical information about World War II. Thus, the team themselves had to find people who can look at an asset and say: “Element 103 on the periodic table was discovered in 1961, which should not exist in WWII,\" or “this type of tableware does not belong to Axis country.\"</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a251af45160dcadaefa1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a251af45160dcadaefa1.webp\" width=\"637\" height=\"351\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/846ec4c925d918aa5d80.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/846ec4c925d918aa5d80.webp\" width=\"380\" height=\"337\" style=\"--source-width:380px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Pressure makes diamonds</h3>\n\n<p>The intimate nature of VR brings as many opportunities as it does difficulties. Now, putting players in the shoes of a WWII soldier wandering in a forest means letting them interact with not only their equipment and enemies but also their surroundings. Some people may try hiding in a bush, and if that bush were a standard 3D asset, their immersion would fade away due to the lack of detail. Or people will pick up an object and try to view it from all angles, which is not common in regular 3D games.</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, they went to UnReal Academy to study VR thoroughly to avoid any potential mistakes.</p>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>Your browser does not support the HTML5 video element</p></td><td><p>Your browser does not support the HTML5 video element</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Your browser does not support the HTML5 video element</p></td><td><p>Your browser does not support the HTML5 video element</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<h3>Limitations breed creativity</h3>\n\n<p>The team did not hesitate to propose ideas to the client if it meant improved quality and historical accuracy. The artist was always pushing the limit by finding better references, and even suggested suitable adjustments to the client’s references. For the record, the client approved half of the ideas. It cost them extra mandates, but the effort was well-received and -appreciated. They treated the project like their own project.</p>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>The incorrect gray box</strong></p>\n<p>During the making of one level, the team found out that the graybox provided didn’t match a photo they found online. They showed it to the client and asked if they could change it. And they could.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3aee812155ef81b8ecb1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3aee812155ef81b8ecb1.webp\" width=\"598\" height=\"370\" style=\"--source-width:340px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>The forest</strong></p>\n<p>The Battle of Hürtgen Forest was the longest battle on the German ground during WWII; it is also the longest single battle the U.S. Army has ever fought. The client wanted to recreate the forest where it took place, with the player glancing at it on top of a hill. However, the pitch-black colour of this area wouldn’t let people see things with great clarity. Therefore, the team suggested they move the viewpoint from the top to the valley of the hill. And again, they were able to move it.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6b7bc99b720bb6d96274.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6b7bc99b720bb6d96274.webp\" width=\"587\" height=\"462\" style=\"--source-width:340px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<h3>A joint effort</h3>\n\n<p>The first piece of advice from the artist is to use Confluence and JIRA to manage your project from the beginning. Moreover, if you are at the helm, you must have strong administration over your partners — both vendors and other studios in-house.</p>\n\n<p>Below are some actions the studio took to advance the development simultaneously:</p>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Action</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>Execution</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>Person in charge (AD/AD/TA/TL?)</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>Channels </strong><strong><br></strong><strong>(Offline, Email, Internal servers, Confluence, Slack, etc.?)</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>Result</strong><strong><br></strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Share assets and materials to every studio (including the vendors)</p></td><td><p>CD</p></td><td><p>PD of Chengdu</p></td><td><p>Several centralized maps called <em>Markets</em></p></td><td><p>This approach saved a lot estimates</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Document findings and update wiki pages</p></td><td><p>CD</p></td><td><p>AD, TL, and lead artists</p></td><td><p>Wiki website</p></td><td><p>Due to the limitations of website access, the result was not as good as they expected</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Invite other teams to Chengdu for a live training program</p></td><td><p>CD</p></td><td><p>AD, TL, and lead artists</p></td><td><p>On-site</p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p>Review all assets before submitting to the client</p></td><td><p>CD</p></td><td><p>AD, TL, and lead artists</p></td><td><p>They got the files from stream p4 and review it</p></td><td>\n<p>→ The client didn't need to review low-quality submissions</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<h3>Inside the studio</h3>\n\n<p>As for internal management, the artist employed an elaborate routine to keep everyone on the same page. Below are some actions his team took:</p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><ol><li><strong>Weekly TL meeting</strong>: to evaluate task progress and to share case studies</li><li><strong>Daily Standups</strong> with TLs, acting leads, and TAs</li><li><strong>Weekly sharing session</strong>:<br>- First, the client shared with us some MMO game knowledge<br>- Then, we keep the conversation going as they are very willing to share more<br>- Now, they realize that we are capable of doing some design work → this has become a routine<br>- This is the loop: ADs receive sharing from client → share it with core team members → core members share it with the whole team<br></li><li><strong>Weekly Q&amp;A</strong>:<br>- Document knowledge to share with key members<br>- Key members spread the knowledge to the whole team<br>- ADs create quizzes about the knowledge to ensure that their team members are learning properly</li><li><strong>Tutorials with subtitles created by TLs</strong>: share the art and technical knowledge gained from this project with everyone</li><li><strong>The book of solution written by the TA team</strong>: help artists understand the basics of optimization, thus saving time for them and TAs</li></ol></div>\n\n<p>The key takeaway is there are always new things to learn. Thus, as creative minds, we always have to:<br></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><ol><li>Make sure knowledge is <strong>recorded</strong></li><li>Make sure knowledge is <strong>shared</strong></li><li>Make sure knowledge is <strong>absorbed</strong></li></ol></div>\n\n<h3>Quality first</h3>\n\n<p>“We strive for a level where every angle is perfect,”.</p>\n\n<p>Reviewing was a constant for the project (and it still is). While the development cycle (2.5 years and counting) appeared daunting at first, it also gave a lot of room for improvement. The teams often look back and identify their shortcomings to raise the quality bar every time. Therefore, the quality rose steadily over time.</p>\n\n<p>To further ensure the quality, the team assembled a group of experienced artists (called firefighters) whose main responsibilities were:</p>\n\n<ul><li>Helping when a vendor who is about to miss the deadline</li><li>Helping when an asset is still not good enough after many rounds of feedback</li></ul>\n\n<p>Those firefighters are <strong>artistically sensitive</strong>, <strong>UE4-savvy</strong>, and <strong>strong-willed</strong> artists. Their contribution to this project cannot be overstated.</p>\n\n<h3>Closing</h3>\n\n<p>Although the project was the team’s first attempt at building full VR environments, we had collectively delivered over 70 levels to the client. This result came from everyone’s commitment to quality. In the end, we think the countless times spent on reviewing the assets and restless days of learning and sharing (including the many Saturdays and Sundays dedicated to making tutorials for new artists) are worth it.</p>\n\n<p>As the project is still in development, we can only wish them the best of luck. These are three major lessons we want to highlight:</p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><ul><li>Review your products constantly</li><li>Promote mutual learning and sharing in and out of your team</li><li>Treat every project like your own</li></ul></div>\n\n<p>Thank you for reading, and happy creating!</p>";
export const images = ["assets/library-inline/3aee812155ef81b8ecb1.webp", "assets/library-inline/6b7bc99b720bb6d96274.webp", "assets/library-inline/736b6fa705c676eb3975.webp", "assets/library-inline/846ec4c925d918aa5d80.webp", "assets/library-inline/a251af45160dcadaefa1.webp", "assets/library-inline/aab0e5546b6e81436086.webp"];
